# =============================================
# BMM Classes (34 elements)
# =============================================

# --- ServiceConcept ---

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/CustomerSegment
ontara-bmm:CustomerSegment rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/ont00000300> ;
    rdfs:label "Customer Segment"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A defined group of customers with shared needs and willingness to pay."@en ;
    skos:definition "A group of people your service is designed for — people who share similar needs and reasons for choosing you. When you define your customer segments, the system uses them to connect your value propositions, pricing, and channels to the right audiences."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ValueProposition
ontara-bmm:ValueProposition rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Value Proposition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A promise of value to a customer segment — what they get and why it matters."@en ;
    skos:definition "The core promise your service makes to a particular group of customers — what they get from you and why they’d choose you over the alternatives. When you define a value proposition, the system holds it as the anchor that your activities, resources, and pricing all exist to deliver."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ServiceOffering
ontara-bmm:ServiceOffering rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Service Offering"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A packaged unit of service that can be priced, resourced, and delivered to customers."@en ;
    skos:definition "A distinct service that your business offers — something a customer can choose, pay for, and receive. When you define a service offering, the system connects it to the activities required to deliver it, the resources it consumes, and the price the customer pays."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/Channel
ontara-bmm:Channel rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/ont00000323> ;
    rdfs:label "Channel"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A way that customers find, reach, or receive your service."@en ;
    skos:definition "A way that customers find you, reach you, or receive your service — your website, a referral network, a walk-in counter, an app. When you define your channels, the system tracks which customer segments each channel serves and how your service offerings are delivered through them."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/DifferentiationClaim
ontara-bmm:DifferentiationClaim rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "Differentiation Claim"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An explicit, testable statement of how your service differs from alternatives."@en ;
    skos:definition "An explicit statement of how your service differs from the alternatives, from the customer’s perspective. Each claim should be testable — something you can back up with evidence. When you define differentiation claims, the system records them as commitments your service is designed to fulfil."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ServiceSubject
ontara-bmm:ServiceSubject rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000023> ;
    rdfs:label "Service Subject"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The entity upon which a service is performed — a person, animal, object, or organisation that receives the service."@en ;
    skos:definition "The thing your service acts on — the dog being groomed, the patient receiving care, the garment being cleaned, the item being repaired. A service subject may have needs independent of whoever commissioned the service, and governance obligations may attach to the subject directly. When you identify your service subjects, the system tracks what is being served, in what condition, and with what independent requirements — separately from who is paying or making decisions."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ServiceParticipant
ontara-bmm:ServiceParticipant rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000023> ;
    rdfs:label "Service Participant"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An entity involved in a service engagement in a defined role — customer, payer, commissioner, decision-maker, or any other role."@en ;
    skos:definition "Any person or organisation involved in a service engagement in a defined role. The person who pays, the organisation that commissions, the carer who makes decisions, the expert called in for assessment — any entity with influence on how the service proceeds. When you identify your service participants, the system tracks who is involved, in what capacity, and how their status affects the service workflow. The set of possible roles is open — the model does not constrain what kinds of participation your service may involve."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/CatalogueEntry
ontara-bmm:CatalogueEntry rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "Catalogue Entry"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A record of something your business has decided to offer, with its price and status."@en ;
    skos:definition "A record of something your business has decided to offer — whether that’s a menu item, a treatment, a course, or any other unit you sell or provide. The catalogue entry connects what the thing intrinsically is to how your business offers it: its price, availability, and status. When you add a catalogue entry, the system tracks it as part of your active offering."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ExternalReference
ontara-bmm:ExternalReference rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "External Reference"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A link to authoritative knowledge that lives outside your system."@en ;
    skos:definition "A link to knowledge that lives outside your system — a supplier datasheet, a regulatory standard, a clinical guideline, an accreditation body. The system doesn’t own this information; it points to it, so you always know where authoritative external knowledge sits in relation to your service."@en .

# --- ActivityModel ---

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ActivityType
ontara-bmm:ActivityType rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Activity Type"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A category of work that your service performs — a named, measurable kind of doing."@en ;
    skos:definition "A named, measurable kind of work that your service performs. Whether it’s a consultation, a grooming session, a dry-cleaning cycle, or a training module — each activity type defines what is done, roughly how long it takes, and how frequently it occurs. When you define your activity types, the system uses them to connect your service delivery to resources, costs, and capacity."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ActivityRecord
ontara-bmm:ActivityRecord rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "Activity Record"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A recorded instance of an activity — what happened, when, and the outcome."@en ;
    skos:definition "A recorded instance of an activity that actually happened — when it occurred, what was done, the outcome, and any notes. Activity records are how the system knows what your service has actually delivered, as opposed to what it planned to deliver. When the system records an activity, it connects to the activity type, the resources involved, and the cost allocation."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ActivityBudget
ontara-bmm:ActivityBudget rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Activity Budget"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A planned volume of activity over a time period — your forward-looking capacity commitment."@en ;
    skos:definition "A forward-looking plan of how much activity you expect to perform over a period. It connects an activity type to a target volume, a time period, and the assumptions behind the estimate. When you set an activity budget, the system uses it as a reference point for resource planning, cost projection, and variance analysis."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ActivityGranularity
ontara-bmm:ActivityGranularity rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Activity Granularity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A policy declaring how finely your service tracks its activities."@en ;
    skos:definition "A policy decision about how precisely your service tracks what it does — do you log every customer interaction, or just completed engagements? Do you distinguish between different types of consultation, or treat them as one? Activity granularity is a design choice, not a given. When you set it, the system calibrates its tracking and reporting to that level of detail."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ActivityCostAllocation
ontara-bmm:ActivityCostAllocation rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "Activity Cost Allocation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The mapping from a type of activity to its financial cost — how much it costs to do this kind of work."@en ;
    skos:definition "The mapping from a type of activity to its financial cost. It connects the doing (activity types) to the spending (cost drivers, revenue). When the system allocates costs to activities, it can answer questions like ‘what does it cost us to deliver a grooming session?’ or ‘how much of our spend goes on consultations vs. administration?’"@en .

# --- ResourcePlanning ---

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ResourceType
ontara-bmm:ResourceType rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Resource Type"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A category of resource that your service requires — personnel, technology, estate, or consumables."@en ;
    skos:definition "A category of resource your service needs — the kind of person, technology, space, or consumable that enables delivery. When you define resource types, the system uses them to connect your capacity planning to the people, equipment, and infrastructure that make your service work."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ResourceInstance
ontara-bmm:ResourceInstance rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/ont00001017> ;
    rdfs:label "Resource Instance"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A planning-level archetype of a specific resource — 'a prescribing clinician at 0.5 FTE'."@en ;
    skos:definition "A specific resource configuration for planning purposes — not a named individual, but a defined slot: a prescribing clinician at 0.5 FTE, a grooming table at full availability, a consultation room shared with another service. When you define resource instances, the system tracks what is available, at what capacity, and what it costs."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/Capability
ontara-bmm:Capability rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/ont00000323> ;
    rdfs:label "Capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An organised ability to produce a defined service function — what your service can do."@en ;
    skos:definition "An organised ability your service has to produce a defined outcome — the right combination of people, equipment, space, and process to deliver a specific function. When you define capabilities, the system maps them to the resources that enable them and the strategic objectives they serve."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/CapacityModel
ontara-bmm:CapacityModel rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "Capacity Model"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A model of how your resource configuration translates into throughput capacity."@en ;
    skos:definition "An assertion about how your resource configuration translates into throughput — how many engagements per week, how many concurrent patients, how many items processed per shift. When you define a capacity model, the system uses it to detect bottlenecks, project future capacity needs, and connect resource planning to activity budgets."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ResourceConstraint
ontara-bmm:ResourceConstraint rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "Resource Constraint"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A limit on resource availability — regulatory, practical, or contractual."@en ;
    skos:definition "A limit on what resources are available — whether imposed externally (regulation, contract, physical law) or declared internally (policy, practical reality). When you record a resource constraint, the system factors it into capacity planning and flags it when plans exceed available resources."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/InventoryRecord
ontara-bmm:InventoryRecord rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000109> ;
    rdfs:label "Inventory Record"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A point-in-time record of how much you have of something."@en ;
    skos:definition "A snapshot of how much stock you have of something — measured at a specific point in time, against a specific item. Inventory records close the loop between what you planned (catalogue, budget) and what you actually have (counted, measured, observed). When the system records inventory, it connects to catalogue entries and resource constraints."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ObjectiveCapabilityMapping
ontara-bmm:ObjectiveCapabilityMapping rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "Objective–Capability Mapping"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A declared link between a strategic objective and the capabilities required to achieve it."@en ;
    skos:definition "A declared traceability link between a strategic objective and the capabilities your service needs to achieve it. When you map objectives to capabilities, the system can answer: ‘do we have the capabilities to achieve this objective?’ and ‘which objectives are at risk if this capability degrades?’"@en .

# --- FinancialPlanning ---

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/RevenueStream
ontara-bmm:RevenueStream rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Revenue Stream"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A mechanism by which your service generates income."@en ;
    skos:definition "A way your business earns money — whether per-episode fees, subscriptions, block contracts, or ancillary income. When you define your revenue streams, the system connects them to the service offerings they monetise, the customer segments they draw from, and the pricing models they apply."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/CostDriver
ontara-bmm:CostDriver rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "Cost Driver"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A structural factor that generates cost in your service."@en ;
    skos:definition "A factor that generates cost in your service — a resource type, an activity pattern, a regulatory requirement, or an operational overhead. When you identify cost drivers, the system connects them to the resources and activities that incur the cost, so you can see where money goes and why."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/UnitEconomics
ontara-bmm:UnitEconomics rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "Unit Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The financial profile of one unit of service — revenue, cost, and margin per engagement."@en ;
    skos:definition "The financial picture of one unit of your service — what it costs to deliver one engagement, what revenue it generates, and what margin remains. When you define unit economics, the system uses them as the building block for financial projections, break-even analysis, and pricing decisions."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/PricingModel
ontara-bmm:PricingModel rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Pricing Model"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The logic that determines what a customer pays for your service."@en ;
    skos:definition "The rules and logic that determine what a customer pays — fixed fees, tiered pricing, time-based rates, outcome-based payments, or any other pricing structure. When you define a pricing model, the system connects it to the service offerings it prices and the revenue streams it feeds."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/FinancialProjection
ontara-bmm:FinancialProjection rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Financial Projection"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A forward-looking financial picture — projected revenue, costs, and margins over time."@en ;
    skos:definition "A forward-looking financial picture for your service — projected revenue, costs, and margins built from your unit economics, activity budgets, and growth assumptions. When you create a financial projection, the system assembles it from your actual model data, not from a disconnected spreadsheet."@en .

# --- GovernanceMapping ---

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/GovernanceRequirement
ontara-bmm:GovernanceRequirement rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000005> ;
    rdfs:label "Governance Requirement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A regulatory, legal, or policy obligation that your service must satisfy."@en ;
    skos:definition "A specific obligation your service must satisfy — a regulation, a professional standard, a contractual requirement, an internal policy commitment. When you record governance requirements, the system tracks them as constraints on your service design and connects them to the evidence that demonstrates compliance."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/AuditEvidenceRecord
ontara-bmm:AuditEvidenceRecord rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "Audit Evidence Record"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A record that demonstrates compliance with a governance requirement."@en ;
    skos:definition "A piece of evidence that demonstrates you are meeting a specific governance requirement — a completed checklist, a signed attestation, a test result, a policy document with a review date. When you record audit evidence, the system connects it to the governance requirement it satisfies."@en .

# --- StakeholderModel ---

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/StakeholderRelationship
ontara-bmm:StakeholderRelationship rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000031> ;
    rdfs:label "Stakeholder Relationship"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A typed, ongoing relationship between your service and an external entity."@en ;
    skos:definition "A typed, ongoing relationship between your service and an external entity — a supplier, a partner, a regulator, a funder, a community group. When you define stakeholder relationships, the system tracks the nature of the relationship, its criticality, and how it connects to your service delivery."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/CooperativeArrangement
ontara-bmm:CooperativeArrangement rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Cooperative Arrangement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A formalised agreement for shared service delivery with another organisation."@en ;
    skos:definition "A formalised agreement between your service and another organisation for shared delivery — joint clinics, shared staff, reciprocal referral agreements, service-level agreements. When you define cooperative arrangements, the system tracks the terms, responsibilities, and expected outcomes of the partnership."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ReferralPathway
ontara-bmm:ReferralPathway rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Referral Pathway"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A structured route by which people or work items flow between your service and others."@en ;
    skos:definition "A defined route by which people or work items flow between your service and another — inbound referrals from GPs, outbound referrals to specialists, or bidirectional flows with partner organisations. When you define referral pathways, the system tracks direction, criteria, protocol, and expected response times."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ExternalDependency
ontara-bmm:ExternalDependency rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030> ;
    rdfs:label "External Dependency"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A record of something your service relies on that you do not control."@en ;
    skos:definition "A record of something your service relies on that you do not control — a supplier, a data feed, an accreditation body, a funding decision. When you record external dependencies, the system tracks the nature of the reliance, its criticality, and what would happen if it failed or changed."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/CommunityRelationship
ontara-bmm:CommunityRelationship rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000031> ;
    rdfs:label "Community Relationship"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Your service’s constitutive connection to a community it serves or belongs to."@en ;
    skos:definition "Your service’s constitutive connection to a community — the patient community, the local area, a professional network, a cultural group. This is not a transactional relationship; it’s the fabric your service exists within. When you define community relationships, the system tracks how your service gives back, listens, and maintains its social licence to operate."@en .

### https://ontara.dev/ontology/bmm/ParticipationModel
ontara-bmm:ParticipationModel rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104> ;
    rdfs:label "Participation Model"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A specification of how customers or patients participate in your service."@en ;
    skos:definition "A specification of how the people your service serves participate in it — what decisions they make, what information they contribute, how much self-service is available, and how their voice shapes the service. When you define a participation model, the system tracks the scope and depth of participation as a design commitment, not an afterthought."@en .

